regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:4:7n-4 VERDICT: LIVE (dev_vme.c unchanged since the upstream import; row `vmedefaultkill` open, no entries, no commit). The FAULT ruling binds the population it was argued on -- population 1 -- and REACHES NEITHER of the other two: population 2 is CONTRADICTED by the ruling's own warrant, because the one in-tree guest (OpenBSD 2.2, `_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/mvme88k/dev/vme.c`) never badaddr-probes the VME2 window and DOES make six unconditional, non-probe, word-width accesses to uncased header-named registers (MASTERCTL r/w, MASTER3/MASTER4/MASTER4MOD w at attach; IRQSTAT r, IRQCLR w in the abort handler) that a bus error turns from today's host kill into a guest `panictrap()` (trap.c:368-369) -- so population 2 goes BACK TO THE OWNER with that reading, with the file's own sibling idiom (DECLINE + latched announce, the #501 shape) as the default pending re-ruling and BACKED ACCEPT NOT licensed; population 3 is a WIDTH question the ruling never touched, "serve the containing word" is REFUTED (lane-wrong on big-endian, whole-word clobber, T1COUNT read side effect -- the seat's own body said so), and it is FILED as its own row with the FAULT round disclosing it in its contract and pinning the interim behaviour with one channel row (the `crimectllane` precedent). Bound: one re-ruling, two filed rows, zero new rows per offset.
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:13:| ledger row `vmedefaultkill` (`ledger.json:45538-45544`, READ) | `state: open`, no `entries`, no `commits`. The owner ruling (2026-09-11 FAULT), the codex design pass (`regress/artifacts/vmedefaultkill/codex_design_20260911.txt`) and the fault-reopen pass (`codex_faultreopen_20260911.txt`, 13,858 b, verdict line 1: *"FAULT (`return 0`) for the unsupported-access fallback; handle register byte lanes separately"*) are all in the row's note. Nothing after 2026-09-11. |
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:16:| the question's evidence at HEAD | the ten case labels, the four storing cases, the `odata` preload at `:65-66`, the VBR seed `0x67000000` at `:161-162`, the 4 KiB `DM_DEFAULT` registration at `:164-166` -- all as the row describes. |
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:35:### 3.1 Population 1 -- the FAULT ruling is UPHELD, as "an access the emulator cannot service"
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:37:Every warrant the owner's ruling rests on is intact here: `memory_rw.c:595-624` converts `return 0` to `M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS` (READ); the same MVME187 constructor arm installs `dev_unreadable` three times for OMITTED hardware (`machine_mvme88k.c:106, :120, :123`), which the seat sharpened into "faults for functionality omitted from emulation"; and the only in-tree guest access to this region is none (vme.c touches offsets <= 0x88; `vmematch` sets `ca_size = 0x100`). The seat's refusal stands and must stay in the record: this is a statement about the EMULATED transaction, never about the real chip (which the local manual cannot describe -- ELSE #2). Nothing to re-rule.
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:39:### 3.2 Population 2 -- NOT reached; the warrant is contradicted; back to the owner with the evidence
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:41:**The ruling's stated model of the access is a PROBE.** The ledger note: *"the guest takes the bus error that `badaddr()` probing expects"*; the row title: *"a guest probing an unimplemented VME2 register"*. The seat conditioned its FAULT choice on exactly that model and said what would weaken it (`codex_faultreopen:26, :30`): *"For a driver operating outside a probe, the cost is substantially greater. The exception can abort an operation or ... stop the guest"* and *"A legitimate driver operation could therefore receive an exception solely because this emulator is incomplete."* The seat could not test it -- the guest source was outside its five permitted files -- and nobody else did.
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:56:**So the measurable consequence of FAULT on population 2 is:** the in-tree guest's `vme0` attach, which today ends the HOST at `vme.c:452` (MASTERCTL read -> `default:` -> `exit(1)`), would instead end the GUEST at the same instruction. That is strictly better than process death and strictly worse than the alternative the file's own siblings already implement -- and it was never put to the owner. *This is a reading; no mvme187 boot runs in this harness (`dev_pcc2.c:92-93`), so the rung-3 probe the row already owes is where it becomes a measurement.*
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:60:**The sibling-handler test, which the option-set rule mandates and which settles the DEFAULT:** the five cased-but-unimplemented registers in this very file (GCSRCTL, TCR, IRQEN, IRQL3, IRQL4) DECLINE with an announce and `break` to the common tail. Population 2 is the same class of register without a case label. FAULT-for-population-2 would make MASTERCTL (0x28) fault while GCSRCTL (0x2c, the next word) declines, with the line between them drawn by which labels upstream happened to type. And FAULT-for-ALL-unimplemented is refuted by the same driver one line later: `:496 ctl = vme2->vme2_gcsrctl` is a cased read that would then panic too. `#501` on the same board resolved the identical shape -- eighteen NAMED registers the model does not implement -- as **member 3, DECLINE + latched announce per offset, NOT member 6**, and its fable5 adjudication's test was *"the fix refuses NOTHING the in-tree guest does"* (`:9730`).
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:62:**Ruling on population 2:** the owner's FAULT ruling does NOT govern it. It is RE-PUT to the owner with the `vme.c` reading above inlined (it is one paragraph and six line citations). Pending that re-ruling the default is the file's and the board's own idiom: DECLINE + latched announce per offset, reads `odata = 0` set explicitly in the arm (the seat's finding (A): the preload at `:65-66` otherwise serves the containing word), writes leave `d->reg[]` untouched.
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:64:**Being harder on the queue author's leaning -- BACKED ACCEPT is NOT licensed, and "available under the addition licence" has it backwards.** The licence demands a MEASURED DELTA or a TRANSFERRED WARRANT. There is no measurement (no boot runs). The only candidate warrant is the driver's RMW of MASTERCTL -- and a backed MASTERCTL buys the guest nothing it can use, because every VME window it would program maps onto `unreadable` space (`machine_mvme88k.c:120, :123`). Meanwhile the population contains registers a blanket store MISREPRESENTS: `vme.h:228-229` marks SETSOFTIRQ as *"VME2_IRQ_SWx only"* and IRQCLR as a clear register, IRQSTAT is status -- the same "cased siblings contradict a blanket store" warrant that defeated member 6 at `#501` (`:9314-9316`), and the design seat's own candidate (d) verdict: *"Invents persistent, RAM-like register semantics. Firmware can then rely on fabricated state."* If the owner wants the master-map registers backed, that is a per-register IMPLEMENT decision (member 7, four registers), licensed by the driver reading -- not a population-wide BACKED ACCEPT.
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:68:**Not reached:** the ruling's contract is "an access the emulator cannot service." A byte at 0x51 is an access the emulator can service -- the datum is in `d->reg[0x14]` -- and merely does not DECODE. That is the `#337`/`#497`/`#529`/`pcc2widthkills` class (an omitted WIDTH of a present register), which this project has disposed four times and never once by FAULT.
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:78:**Disposition:** population 3 is its OWN ROW (name is the main loop's; the shape is `crimectllane`/`pcc2widecompose`). The reopening rule forbids folding it into the FAULT round: different semantic class (width vs. unimplemented register) and a DIFFERENT ORACLE (no exception + unchanged store + one announce, versus an exception). The FAULT round therefore:
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:80:* carries ONE row that pins that interim behaviour and is NAMED as a channel the width row will red and re-derive (exactly how `#529`'s `sw08/sh08` rows are recorded in `crimectllane`);
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:81:* records the warrant for deferring rather than repairing: the in-tree guest makes NO sub-word VME2 access (every `vme2reg` field is `u_long`; no `vme2_` reference exists outside `dev/vme.[ch]` -- grep), so FAULT there breaks no in-tree guest path, which population 2 cannot say.
regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_4.md:86:> **A disposition ruling binds the population it was argued on.** When an arm is later shown to mix populations, each population not presented is tested against the ruling's STATED WARRANTS, never against its conclusion: a population the warrants reach is covered without re-ruling; a population a warrant CONTRADICTS goes back to the owner with the contradicting evidence inlined; a population the warrants do not touch gets its own row. The partition is read off the code -- the header, the switch, the access width -- so it is finite, and the obligation is one re-ruling or one row PER POPULATION, never per offset.
